Wait — the raw text `{"a":null,"b":null,"c":null,"d":null}` — would the OLD code (sanitize first) have mangled it? sanitize regex: `(?:[ \t]*(?:null|undefined|NaN)){4,}[ \t]*` — requires 4 consecutive nulls separated only by spaces/tabs. In `{"a":null,"b":null,...}` between nulls there are `","` chars → not matched. Hmm! So the null-run regex wouldn't fire here.

What WOULD the old code mangle? `[ \t]{2,}` → ' ' (double spaces inside JSON string values) and `(.)\1{40,}` (40+ same char). So a better test: `{"text":"a  b"}` (two spaces) — old: sanitize → `{"text":"a b"}` → data corrupted. New: parses raw → 'a  b' preserved. That's a clean behavioral test of the fix.

Let me use: `{"a":"x  y","b":"--------...40+"}`. Simpler: `{"text":"раз  два"}` → expect exactly 'раз  два'.

